    ],
    "reading_order": 13,
    "text": "So far, in all our examples, we used direct arguments to establish the truth of the\nresults. We now explore ‘indirect’ arguments, in particular, a very powerful tool in\nmathematics known as ‘proof by contradiction’. We have already used this method in\nChapter 1 to establish the irrationality of several numbers and also in other chapters to\nprove some theorems. Here, we do several more examples to illustrate the idea.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 17,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p017_b14",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.01,
      335.73,
      420.03,
      377.03
    ],
    "reading_order": 14,
    "text": "Before we proceed, let us explain what a contradiction is. In mathematics, a\ncontradiction occurs when we get a statement p such that p is true and ~p, its negation,\nis also true. For example,",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 17,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p017_b15",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.02,
      383.32,
      240.02,
      411.36
    ],
    "reading_order": 15,
    "text": "p: \na\nx\nb\n\n, where a and b are coprime.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 17,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p017_b16",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.01,
      411.33,
      198.73,
      423.59
    ],
    "reading_order": 16,
    "text": "q: 2 divides both ‘a’ and ‘b’.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 17,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p017_b17",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      48.0,
      430.53,
      420.03,
      490.79
    ],
    "reading_order": 17,
    "text": "If we assume that p is true and also manage to show that q is true, then we have\narrived at a contradiction, because q implies that the negation of p is true. If you\nremember, this is exactly what happened when we tried to prove that \n2 is irrational\n(see Chapter 1).",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
    "document_id": "NCERT_X_MATH_JEMH1A1",
    "page": 17,
    "block_id": "jemh1a1_p017_b18",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "bbox": [
      72.0,
      496.53,
      420.07,
      508.79
    ],
    "reading_order": 18,
    "text": "How does proof by contradiction work? Let us see this through a specific example.",
    "latex": null,
    "image_ref": null,
    "confidence": 1.0
  },
  {
